    "title": "Today's NYT Connections Hints (and Answer) for Sunday, April 5, 2026",
    "text": "If you’re looking for the Connections answer for Sunday, April 5, 2026, read on—I’ll share some clues, tips, and strategies, and finally the solutions to all four categories. Along the way, I’ll explain the meanings of the trickier words and we’ll learn how everything fits together. Beware, there are spoilers below for April 5, NYT Connections #1,029! Read on if you want some hints (and then the answer) to today’s Connections game. If you want an easy way to come back to our Connections hints every day, bookmark this page. (If you play Wordle, Strands, or Quordle, check out our hints for those games, too.) Below, I’ll give you some oblique hints at today’s Connections answers. And farther down the page, I’ll reveal the themes and the answers. Scroll slowly and take just the hints you need! Credit: Connections/NYT The category types in today’s Connections puzzleHere are spoiler-free hints that describe the type of each category in today’s Connections:Yellow category - Related nounsGreen category - Parts of a wholeBlue category - Related nounsPurple category - WordplayHints for the themes in today’s Connections puzzleHere are some spoiler-free hints for the groupings in today’s Connections:Yellow category - You learned these in chemistry class.Green category - Associated with a mystery solver.Blue category - They all relate to the same verb.Purple category - They all have icky-sticky sounding words in them.BEWARE: Spoilers follow for today’s Connections puzzle!We’re about to give away some of the answers. Scroll slowly if you don’t want the whole thing spoiled. (The full solution is a bit further down.)A heads up about the tricky partsSHELL, MUSHROOM, COIN, and PIPE might all make you think of Mario, but those all belong to separate categories today.THE BIRD is another way of saying “the middle finger.”PIPE is something used to smoke tobacco.PULPIT starts with the word “pulp.” What are the categories in today’s Connections?Yellow: ATOMIC STRUCTURE TERMSGreen: PARTS OF A SHERLOCK HOLMES COSTUMEBlue: THINGS TO FLIPPurple: STARTING WITH SYNONYMS FOR “SLUSH”DOUBLE BEWARE: THE SOLUTION IS BELOWReady to learn the answers to today’s Connections puzzle? I give them all away below.What are the yellow words in today’s Connections?The yellow grouping is considered to be the most straightforward. The theme for today’s yellow group is ATOMIC STRUCTURE TERMS and the words are: ELECTRON, NUCLEUS, ORBIT, SHELL.What are the green words in today’s Connections?The green grouping is supposed to be the second-easiest. The theme for today’s green category is PARTS OF A SHERLOCK HOLMES COSTUME and the words are: DEERSTALKER, MAGNIFYING GLASS, PIPE, VIOLIN.What are the blue words in today’s Connections?The blue grouping is the second-hardest. The theme for today’s blue category is THINGS TO FLIP and the words are: COIN, LIGHT SWITCH, PANCAKE, THE BIRD.What are the purple words in today’s Connections?The purple grouping is considered to be the hardest. The theme for today’s purple category is STARTING WITH SYNONYMS FOR “SLUSH” and the words are: GOOGOL, MUSHROOM, PASTEURIZE, PULPIT.How I solved today’s ConnectionsPIPE, SHELL, COIN, and MUSHROOM could all be objects from Mario games. It could be a misdirect, but let’s try anyway. Yeah, it was a misdirect.Maybe SHELL goes with ELECTRON, NUCLEUS, and ORBIT as words related to atoms. 🟨 What do you think so far? PIPE, DEERSTALKER, and MAGNIFYING GLASS might go together as words related to Sherlock Holmes?Maybe GOOGOL, PASTEURIZE, PULPIT, and MUSHROOM go together because they all have gooey words at their starts: goo, paste, pulp, mush. 🟪I thought THE BIRD might be a reference to the Hitchcock film, but I don’t see any others. Oh, maybe that goes with COIN, LIGHT SWITCH, and PANCAKE as things one flips: Flip THE BIRD (aka throwing up the middle finger), flip a COIN, flip a LIGHT SWITCH, and flip a PANCAKE. 🟦That means VIOLIN completes the category with PIPE, DEERSTALKER, and MAGNIFYING GLASS. 🟩 I don’t associate a VIOLIN with Sherlock, but if that’s a thing, then so be it!Connections Puzzle #1029 🟩🟨🟪🟦 🟨🟨🟨🟨 🟪🟪🟪🟪 🟦🟦🟦🟦 🟩🟩🟩🟩 How to play ConnectionsI have a full guide to playing Connections, but here’s a refresher on the rules:First, find the Connections game either on the New York Times website or in their Games app (formerly the Crossword app). You’ll see a game board with 16 tiles, each with one word or phrase. Your job is to select a group of four tiles that have something in common. Often they are all the same type of thing (for example: RAIN, SLEET, HAIL, and SNOW are all types of wet weather) but sometimes there is wordplay involved (for example, BUCKET, GUEST, TOP TEN, and WISH are all types of lists: bucket list, guest list, and so on).Select four items and hit the Submit button. If you guessed correctly, the category and color will be revealed. (Yellow is easiest, followed by green, then blue, then purple.) If your guess was incorrect, you’ll get a chance to try again.You win when you’ve correctly identified all four groups. But if you make four mistakes before you finish, the game ends and the answers are revealed.How to win ConnectionsThe most important thing to know to win Connections is that the groupings are designed to be tricky. Expect to see overlapping groups. For example, one puzzle seemed to include six breakfast foods: BACON, EGG, PANCAKE, OMELET, WAFFLE, and CEREAL. But BACON turned out to be part of a group of painters along with CLOSE, MUNCH, and WHISTLER, and EGG was in a group of things that come by the dozen (along with JUROR, ROSE, and MONTH). So don’t hit “submit” until you’ve confirmed that your group of four contains only those four things.If you’re stuck, another strategy is to look at the words that seem to have no connection to the others. If all that comes to mind when you see WHISTLER is the painting nicknamed “Whistler’s Mother,” you might be on to something. When I solved that one, I ended up googling whether there was a painter named Close, because Close didn’t fit any of the obvious themes, either.Another way to win when you’re stuck is, obviously, to read a few helpful hints–which is why we share these pointers every day. Check back tomorrow for the next puzzle! Not the day you're after? Here's the solution to today's Connections.",
